   </property>
   <property name="text">
    <string>&lt;html&gt;&lt;head/&gt;&lt;body&gt;&lt;p&gt;Here is the malting stage in which barley is soaked in water, then left out to germinate, before finally being dried in a kiln.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Soaking the barley involves leaving it in a large vessel of water for 2-3 days, which raises the moisture content to around 45%. &lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Germination is the process of taking the soaked barley and allowing it to grow, developing malt enzymes. The barley is spread out for multiple days, allowing the enzymes to break down the starch of the barley.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Finally, the kilning process begins. Kilning takes the grain and reduces it's moisture content while also stopping the germination process. The heat of the kiln also gives the grain its color. After this process is over, the grain has now become malt.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/body&gt;&lt;/html&gt;</string>
   </property>
